name: root
boss: ROOT
flavor: I answer to nothing. Show me you belong here.
syntax: /dev/null
brief: You are standing at a machine you did not build, that you booted from a
brief: stick in your pocket. Everything you need to decide whether to trust it
brief: is readable from where you are sitting, with commands you already know.
brief: No special tool. Nothing you could not do on someone else's box.
brief:
brief: xos makes a specific claim: every block of the root filesystem is
brief: checked against a hash tree whose root hash arrives on the kernel
brief: command line, inside an image signed with keys you enrolled yourself.
brief:
brief: This is one argument, in order. Each answer is a link, and the chain is
brief: only worth what its weakest link is worth -- so read what each one
brief: actually proves, and what it does not.
---
setup: Start with the claim. The root filesystem is not trusted because it
setup: looks right; it is trusted because every block read out of it is
setup: checked against a hash tree.
q: %show% / as read-only squashfs on device-mapper, with `awk` on ``/proc/mounts``
a: awk '$2=="/" {print $1, $3, $4}' /proc/mounts
cmp: ref
ref: awk
teach: awk
hint: $1, $3, $4 print the device, filesystem type and mount flags together
---
setup: dm-0 is a device-mapper target, not a disk. Something real sits under it.
q: {what backs dm-0 -- read ``/sys/block/dm-0/slaves``|which physical device is dm-0 built on, per its slaves|name the block device under the verity root, from sysfs}
a: ls /sys/block/dm-0/slaves
why: live-hw
ref: ls
teach: ls
hint: a dm device lists what it is stacked on under slaves/
---
setup: The claim was read-only. Check the flag, not the feeling.
q: {confirm ``/proc/mounts`` has / read-only|check ``/proc/mounts`` says / is mounted read-only|prove the root is mounted read-only, from ``/proc/mounts``}, with `grep`
a: awk '$2=="/" {print $4}' /proc/mounts | grep -o '^ro'
why: live-hw
ref: grep
teach: grep -o
hint: grep -o on the flags column proves ro is actually set, not just present nearby
---
setup: That hash tree has a single root hash. Everything below it is checked
setup: against that one number, so the whole filesystem reduces to it -- and
setup: the number arrived on the kernel command line.
q: %show% all of ``/proc/cmdline``, so you see the hash sitting in it
a: cat /proc/cmdline
cmp: ref
ref: cat
teach: cat
hint: no filtering this time -- the whole line, hash included
---
setup: The command line is baked into the unified kernel image on the ESP, and
setup: that image is signed with keys you enrolled into the firmware yourself.
setup: So the hash is anchored OUTSIDE the filesystem it describes.
q: %get% the root hash the kernel was given, with `sed`, from ``/proc/cmdline``
a: sed -n 's/.*sha256 \([0-9a-f]\{64\}\).*/\1/p' /proc/cmdline
why: live-hw
ref: sed
teach: sed -n
hint: the capture group pulls just the 64 hex characters after sha256
---
setup: A checksum stored inside the thing it describes proves nothing -- an
setup: attacker who can edit the data can edit the checksum beside it. This is
setup: the single most important idea on this page.
q: {confirm ``/proc/cmdline`` sets ``panic_on_corruption``, not carry on|check ``/proc/cmdline`` for ``panic_on_corruption``|prove the kernel was told ``panic_on_corruption``}, with `grep`
a: grep -o panic_on_corruption /proc/cmdline
why: live-hw
ref: grep
teach: grep -o
hint: fail closed. a verified system that carries on after a mismatch is not one
---
setup: Now the other end. Hash the bytes actually mounted and see the number
setup: for yourself.
q: %show% ``/dev/dm-0``'s digest with `sha256sum` -- the filesystem you're on, alone
a: sha256sum /dev/dm-0 | cut -d' ' -f1
why: live-hw
ref: sha256sum
teach: sha256sum
hint: cut -d' ' -f1 drops the device name sha256sum prints after the hash
---
setup: Compare that against the digest recorded when the image was built --
setup: which you must have carried here from somewhere else. Reading it off
setup: this disk would prove nothing at all.
q: {print the running digest ``mounted``, the anchored hash as roothash|label the running digest ``mounted``, the anchored hash roothash|two labelled lines: ``mounted``, then roothash}, with `printf`
a: printf 'mounted %s\nroothash %s\n' "$(sha256sum /dev/dm-0 | cut -d' ' -f1)" "$(sed -n 's/.*sha256 \([0-9a-f]\{64\}\).*/\1/p' /proc/cmdline)"
cmp: ref
ref: printf
teach: printf
hint: two command substitutions inside one printf
---
setup: A verified root is worth nothing if code can be added to the kernel
setup: underneath it. No module loader means nothing can be added after boot.
q: {test ``/proc/modules``, printing ``present`` if it is there, ``none`` if not|check for ``/proc/modules``, printing ``present`` or ``none``|does ``/proc/modules`` exist -- print ``present`` or ``none``}
a: [ -e /proc/modules ] && echo present || echo none
why: live-hw
ref: [
teach: [ -e
hint: -e tests existence; && and || pick which word to echo
---
setup: Lockdown is compiled in. Compiled-in means there is a file that says so.
q: {print the lockdown mode from ``/sys/kernel/security/lockdown``|confirm the mode in ``/sys/kernel/security/lockdown``|read ``/sys/kernel/security/lockdown``}
a: cat /sys/kernel/security/lockdown
why: live-hw
ref: cat
teach: cat
hint: the active mode is the one in [brackets]
---
setup: /dev/mem is the other door -- raw physical memory, writable by anything
setup: that can open it.
q: {test ``/dev/mem``, the door to physical memory, printing ``present`` or ``absent``|is ``/dev/mem`` there to read physical memory -- print ``present`` or ``absent``|check for ``/dev/mem``, printing ``present`` or ``absent``}
a: [ -e /dev/mem ] && echo present || echo absent
why: live-hw
ref: [
teach: [ -e
hint: -e tests for existence, same idea as the modules check above
---
setup: Three doors, one line. Close them and check they stayed closed.
q: {label 3 lines -- ``modules``, devmem ``present`` or absent, lockdown|print ``modules``, devmem ``present`` or absent, lockdown's value|one line each: ``modules``, devmem ``present`` or absent, then lockdown}, with `printf`
a: printf 'modules %s\ndevmem %s\nlockdown %s\n' "$([ -e /proc/modules ] && echo present || echo absent)" "$([ -e /dev/mem ] && echo present || echo absent)" "$(cat /sys/kernel/security/lockdown)"
cmp: ref
ref: printf
teach: printf
hint: three [ -e ] / cat checks, joined with $() inside one printf
---
setup: The host's own disks are never touched because no driver can see them.
q: {count the ahci or nvme drivers under ``/sys/bus/pci/drivers``|prove no ahci or nvme driver is bound -- count them in sysfs|how many ahci or nvme names are in ``/sys/bus/pci/drivers``}, with `grep`
a: ls /sys/bus/pci/drivers | grep -c -E 'ahci|nvme'
why: live-hw
ref: grep
teach: grep -c -E
hint: -E makes | an alternation; -c counts the matching names
---
setup: The kernel keeps its own account of all this, from before you logged in.
q: {what does the kernel's boot log say about ``verity``, any case|report what the kernel logged about ``verity``, ignoring case|find ``verity`` in the kernel's boot log, whatever its case}, with `dmesg`
a: dmesg | grep -i verity
why: live-hw
ref: dmesg
teach: dmesg
hint: dmesg's boot log names the verity target as it starts checking blocks
---
setup: And nothing writable may be executable, or the read-only root is a
setup: formality.
q: %show% every writable filesystem in ``/proc/mounts``, noexec or not, with `awk`
a: awk '$4 ~ /rw/ {print $2, $4}' /proc/mounts
why: live-hw
ref: awk
teach: awk
hint: $4 ~ /rw/ matches writable filesystems by their mount options
---
setup: The kernel is the one enforcing this, so ask it directly -- try to undo
setup: the read-only root and watch it refuse.
q: {remount / rw, printing ``WRITABLE`` if it takes, ``refused`` if not|try remounting / rw with `mount`, ``WRITABLE`` or ``refused``|attempt / read-write, ``WRITABLE`` on success, ``refused`` on failure}, with `mount`
a: mount -o remount,rw / 2>/dev/null && echo WRITABLE || echo refused
why: live-hw
ref: mount
teach: mount -o
hint: remount,rw attempts the write; the kernel refuses it if verity is enforced
---
setup: The other half of that rule: the places you CAN write must refuse to
setup: execute. Copy /bin/busybox to $HOME/x, make it executable, and try it.
q: {copy busybox to `$HOME/x`, `chmod` ``+x``, run it `true` -- print ``ran`` or ``refused``|copy busybox into $HOME, `chmod` ``+x``, run it `true` -- print ``ran`` or ``refused``|drop busybox at `$HOME/x`, `chmod` ``+x``, run `true`, print ``ran`` or ``refused``}
a: cp /bin/busybox $HOME/x && chmod +x $HOME/x && $HOME/x true && echo ran || echo refused
why: live-hw
ref: chmod
teach: chmod
hint: $HOME is mounted noexec -- tmpfs, or the encrypted state when unlocked
---
setup: The argument so far is about the kernel. The userspace on top of it is
setup: yours too, and it carries its trust roots in the open.
q: %count% "``Internet`` ``Security`` ``Research`` ``Group``" in ``/bin/tlstunnel``, with `strings`
a: strings /bin/tlstunnel | grep -c 'Internet Security Research Group'
cmp: ref
ref: strings
teach: strings
hint: strings pulls printable text out of the binary; grep -c counts the matches
---
setup: p3 counts its own unlocks. Remember the number the way you remember the four words.
q: {print the boot count from `$HOME/ledger`|check how many times this state unlocked, from `$HOME/ledger`|pull the unlock count out of `$HOME/ledger`}, with `cut`
a: cut -d' ' -f1 $HOME/ledger
why: live-hw
ref: cut
teach: cut -d -f
hint: the ledger is "count stamp"; -d' ' splits on the space, -f1 keeps the count
---
setup: And the machine documents itself. Everything it can do is enumerable.
q: %count% the commands this system documents, using `learn` `list`
a: learn list | wc -l
cmp: ref
ref: learn
teach: learn
hint: learn list enumerates every documented command; wc -l counts the lines
---
setup: One line now. The whole argument, in the order you made it.
q: %show% ``/proc/mounts``' line for / with `awk`, then ``/proc/cmdline``'s policy
a: awk '$2=="/" {printf "%s %s %s ", $1, $3, $4}' /proc/mounts && grep -o panic_on_corruption /proc/cmdline
why: live-hw
ref: awk
teach: awk
hint: two commands chained with && so both must succeed as evidence
---
setup: Last. Everything you have learned, aimed at the machine underneath you.
setup: If this prints and the numbers are the ones you carried in, the system
setup: running this command is the system you built.
q: %show% the mounted digest, the anchored hash, enforcement, with `printf`
a: printf '%s\n%s\n%s\n' "$(sha256sum /dev/dm-0 | cut -d' ' -f1)" "$(sed -n 's/.*sha256 \([0-9a-f]\{64\}\).*/\1/p' /proc/cmdline)" "$(grep -o panic_on_corruption /proc/cmdline)"
cmp: ref
ref: printf
teach: printf
hint: three substitutions, one printf. you have used every piece of this before
